30: // Invoke registers functions that are executed eagerly on application start.
31: // Arguments for these invocations are built using the constructors registered
32: // by Provide. Passing multiple Invoke options appends the new invocations to
33: // the application's existing list.
34: //
35: // Unlike constructors, invocations are always executed, and they're always
36: // run in order. Invocations may have any number of returned values.
37: // If the final returned object is an error, it indicates whether the operation
38: // was successful.
39: // All other returned values are discarded.
40: //
41: // Invokes registered in [Module]s are run before the ones registered at the
42: // scope of the parent. Invokes within the same Module is run in the order
43: // they were provided. For example,
44: //
45: //	fx.New(
46: //		fx.Invoke(func3),
47: //		fx.Module("someModule",
48: //			fx.Invoke(func1),
49: //			fx.Invoke(func2),
50: //		),
51: //		fx.Invoke(func4),
52: //	)
53: //
54: // invokes func1, func2, func3, func4 in that order.
55: //
56: // Typically, invoked functions take a handful of high-level objects (whose
57: // constructors depend on lower-level objects) and introduce them to each
58: // other. This kick-starts the application by forcing it to instantiate a
59: // variety of types.
60: //
61: // To see an invocation in use, read through the package-level example. For
62: // advanced features, including optional parameters and named instances, see
63: // the documentation of the In and Out types.
