31: // Supply provides instantiated values for dependency injection as if
32: // they had been provided using a constructor that simply returns them.
33: // The most specific type of each value (as determined by reflection) is used.
34: //
35: // This serves a purpose similar to what fx.Replace does for fx.Decorate.
36: //
37: // For example, given:
38: //
39: //	type (
40: //		TypeA struct{}
41: //		TypeB struct{}
42: //		TypeC struct{}
43: //	)
44: //
45: //	var a, b, c = &TypeA{}, TypeB{}, &TypeC{}
46: //
47: // The following two forms are equivalent:
48: //
49: //	fx.Supply(a, b, fx.Annotated{Target: c})
50: //
51: //	fx.Provide(
52: //		func() *TypeA { return a },
53: //		func() TypeB { return b },
54: //		fx.Annotated{Target: func() *TypeC { return c }},
55: //	)
56: //
57: // Supply panics if a value (or annotation target) is an untyped nil or an error.
58: //
59: // [Private] can be used to restrict access to supplied values.
60: //
61: // # Supply Caveats
62: //
63: // As mentioned above, Supply uses the most specific type of the provided
64: // value. For interface values, this refers to the type of the implementation,
65: // not the interface. So if you supply an http.Handler, fx.Supply will use the
66: // type of the implementation.
67: //
68: //	var handler http.Handler = http.HandlerFunc(f)
69: //	fx.Supply(handler)
70: //
71: // Is equivalent to,
